  { q: 'How do ravens communicate?', a: 'Ravens communicate through a large vocal repertoire (30+ documented call types), body postures, wing and tail positions, and gestures. Research by Pika & Bugnyar (2011) documented that ravens use pointing-like and showing gestures to direct a partner\'s attention to objects — a referential communication ability previously thought limited to humans and great apes. They also mimic other species and can learn human words.' },
  { q: 'Can ravens use gestures to communicate?', a: 'Yes. Pika & Bugnyar (2011, Current Biology) showed that wild ravens at the Cumberland Wildpark used pointing-like gestures (presenting an object with the beak while watching a partner\'s response) and "showing" (holding an object near a partner). These are referential gestures — the raven is directing attention to something outside itself. This was the first documented use of referential gestures in a non-primate animal.' },
